For a capacitor, the energy stored at any instant is \[W=\frac{1}{2}CV^2\] When connected to an AC source, the capacitor stores energy during part of the cycle and returns the same energy back to the source during the remaining part. Hence, \[\text{Energy absorbed in one cycle}
=
\text{Energy returned in one cycle}\] Therefore, the net energy stored over one complete cycle is 0. Equivalently, the average power absorbed by an ideal capacitor is \[P_{\text{avg}}
=
V_{\text{rms}}I_{\text{rms}}\cos\phi\] For a capacitor current leads voltage by, \(\phi=90^\circ\) Hence, \[P_{\text{avg}}
=
V_{\text{rms}}I_{\text{rms}}\cos90^\circ
=
0\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\textbf{C. } \text{ Zero}}\]

