=
(2+4s)I(s)\] \[I(s)
=
\frac{2.5}{s^2(2+4s)}
=
\frac{0.625}{s^2(s+0.5)}\] Using partial fractions, \[I(s)
=
\frac{A}{s^2}
+
\frac{B}{s}
+
\frac{C}{s+0.5}\] Multiplying by \(s^2(s+0.5)\), \[0.625=A(s+0.5)+Bs(s+0.5)+Cs^2\] Putting \(s=0\), \[A=1.25\] Putting \(s=-0.5\), \[C=2.5\] Also, \[B+C=0 \ \Rightarrow \ B=-2.5\] Hence, \[I(s)
=
\frac{1.25}{s^2}
-\frac{2.5}{s}
+\frac{2.5}{s+0.5}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[i(t)
=
1.25t-2.5+2.5e^{-0.5t}\] \[\boxed{
i(t)
=
\frac{5}{4}t-\frac{5}{2}
+\frac{5}{2}e^{-t/2}
}\] At \(t=4\) s, \[i(4)
=
1.25(4)-2.5+2.5e^{-2}
=
2.838~\text{A}\] \[\boxed{i(4)=2.84~\text{A}}\] Further, \[V_L(s)
=
LsI(s)
=
4sI(s)
=
\frac{5}{s}
-\frac{10}{2s+1}\] Therefore, \[v_L(t)
=
5-5e^{-t/2}
=
5\left(1-e^{-t/2}\right)\] At \(t=4\) s, \[v_L(4)
=
5(1-e^{-2})
=
4.323~\text{V}\] \[\boxed{v_L(4)=4.32~\text{V}}\]

