From the equivalent circuit of a DC series motor, armature current is constant and armature resistance varies with the field connection. For the linear region of the B-H curve, \[\phi \propto NI_{a}\] \[T \propto \phi I_{a}\] The motor takes the rated current in both cases, hence \[I_{a1}=I_{a2}=I\] When both field coils are connected in series, <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[\phi_{1}\propto (NI+NI)=2NI\] When the two field sections are connected in parallel, \[\phi_{2}\propto \left(\frac{NI}{2}+\frac{NI}{2}\right)=NI\] Therefore, \[\frac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}
=
\frac{\phi_{1}}{\phi_{2}}
=
\frac{2NI}{NI}
=
\frac{2}{1}=2\] Now, \[E=k'\phi\omega\] Since \(I_{a}\) is constant, \[E_{1}=V_{t}-I_{a}(R_{f}+R_{f}+R_{a})\] When the two field sections are connected in parallel, <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[E_{2}
=
V_{t}
-
I_{a}
\left(
R_{a}+\frac{R_{f}}{2}
\right)\] Since the net resistance decreases in the parallel connection, \[E_{2}&gt;E_{1}\] Also, \[\frac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}
=
\frac{\phi_{1}\omega_{1}}{\phi_{2}\omega_{2}}\] \[\frac{\omega_{1}}{\omega_{2}}
=
\frac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}
\cdot
\frac{\phi_{2}}{\phi_{1}}
=
\frac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}
\cdot
\frac{NI}{2NI}
=
\frac{E_{1}}{2E_{2}}\] Since \[E_{2}&gt;E_{1}\] \[\frac{\omega_{1}}{\omega_{2}}&lt;1\] Hence \[\boxed{\text{2 and &lt;1}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a DC series motor in the linear magnetic region, \(\phi \propto NI_{a}\).</li><li>Torque is proportional to \(\phi I_{a}\).</li><li>Back EMF is given by \(E=k'\phi\omega\).</li><li>Connecting field sections in parallel reduces field resistance and increases speed.</li></ul></div>

